ned the common theme in  everything that we do up here and that  is the stem field so science technology  engineering and math now the nice thing  is there are a lot of different options  within those fields all of those fields  could lead to a career like this one to  become an astronaut you do have to have  a degree in one of those areas but the  choices are really broad for example I'm  a life scientist a biologist and a  physiologist as I think you already just  saw up here with me on my crew there's  an electrical engineer there's a  military doctor there's a military test  pilot with a background in engineering  and then our two Russian cosmonauts up  here as well with their engineering and  military and backgrounds so all of these  different backgrounds are our basic  paths that you can use to get up here so  spending time in any of those science  technology engineering and math courses  really helped us all get where we are  today hi my name is Naomi nice open  floor 10k and my question is what ever  research they do on the space station  and do you have due any experience  outside the space station  well it's really amazing how many  different types of experiments we  conduct up here and as a scientist it is  so exciting because as you can imagine  once you eliminate these gravity-driven  effects that are always present no  matter where you are on the earth you  might unveil a whole variety of  responses and results that we never even  thought possible so we do experiments  ranging from physiology and medicine how  the spaceflight environment affects our  human bodies we do experiments about  combustion even flames burn differently  in space without gravity without  convection driving that motion flames  burn differently so we study in a  community in a chamber we study how  these how different things burn in terms  of flammability and safety responses  also in terms of fuel efficiency that  might enhance fuel efficiency on earth  and also for future spacecraft we do  biotechnology experiments I did a genome  sequencing a DNA sequencing experiment  called genes in space up here and we do  fluids experiments we do really as I  mentioned any type of experiment and it  is really an amazing playground as a  scientist to be up here and outside the  space station I was fortunate enough to  go outside of the space station three  times now on spacewalks and those are  actually not for scientific experiments  those are for repair jobs we have  Hardware both inside and outside the  space station that's critical to making  sure that all the systems work up here  to accomplish our mission and one of the  things that needed to be upgraded where  our batteries if you've seen a picture  of the space station you can see the  immense very impressive solar arrays we  harness all of the energy that we use up  here all of the power requirements come  from the Sun from solar energy but of  course since we have these multiple  times when we are in Sun and then in  shade throughout the day we also have to  have some efficient batteries to keep  that energy when we're not in direct  sunlight so we conducted a series of  spacewalks to upgrade those batteries to  better more efficient batteries lithium  ion battery it's just like you're using  down on earth as well and that I think  was one of the most amazing  things that I've done while I'm up here  is venturing out of the hatch in a  spacesuit and having really nothing  between you and the earth below except  for you at the visor of your helmet it  really is an extraordinary experience my  name is Bryan Keating I'm in 42nd grade  UC San Diego and I have a question from  Instagram follower one of your millions  of Instagram followers and I remind  folks you can follow Jessica on  instagram and twitter at astro Jessa  underscore Jessica this is actually a  question from one of the scripts in  Instagram followers and her name is Alma  venero and she would like to know  besides coming to UCSD what's the best  advice for young women who want to  become astronauts there does seem to be  a trend with female astronauts and UCSD  it's a great connection but I think the  most important thing to do is to make  sure that you identify your passion and  do what it is that you really care about  once you've done that you do need to  work really hard to make those dreams  come true and it sounds a bit trite when  I say it but it really is true I think  this is proof that your dreams can come  true I've been saying I wanted to be an  astronaut since I was five years old and  because I had identified that as my  passion and also identified other  aspects of science and exploration that  led me down that road and pursued it  with a lot of hard work and dedication  and perseverance and then a lot of luck  all wrapped together to actually get me  where I am today and the point is really  anybody can do it if that's what you set  your mind out to and just to follow up  on that previous question I think I  misunderstood a little bit there are  other experiments outside the space  station there are other instruments like  the Alpha Magnetic Spectrometer  that are measuring high-energy radiation  so looking at things like understanding  and unravelling more about the origins  of the universe and this is actually an  instrument which we just finished  repairing with a series of spacewalks so  it's all connected up here thank you  next up we have a question from well  obstacles did you face and ordered so I  shouldn't be able to  scientists and sorry could you repeat  that one one more time what obstacles  did you face in order to actually be an  scientist okay  obstacles well I think a lot of hard  work a lot of application processes but  just like anything in life things that  are worth it things that give you a  great reward involve a lot of hard work  involve some of those obstacles and  involve failing along the way for me one  of my big failures along the way was  actually in applying for this job it  took me a few different times to apply  before I was accepted and four years  before I started in 2013 I also  interviewed for this job in 2009 I made  it all the way to the final round of  astronaut applicants and the people at  Scripps that are there with you today  remember that well because it was just  at the time where I was finishing my PhD  at Scripps and things went well at the  interview I knew I'd given it my best  shot but only nine people were selected  and I wasn't one of them so that was a  pretty big obstacle to overcome because  I had gone through everything and I  thought well you know I'd failed at it  it didn't happen it didn't work out that  time and so I could have given up then  but I didn't I waited until the next  time that I could apply and when I  applied again this time even though I  thought well it'll be worth applying  just to see but I'm sure it'll be the  same result and I'm gonna go through all  of this again and psychologically and  mentally it's probably going to be quite  difficult but if I don't apply if I give  up now that I'll never know and sure  enough this time it worked out and this  time brought me to where I am today so I  think that's just it was a lesson  learned for me no matter how many  obstacles to end in your way you have to  be ready to persevere and to keep on  trying even if you fail along the way  hello my name is Johanna and was your  space training difficult to master and  if so what part of your training was  both complex to adapt you there are a  lot of aspects of our training that are  difficult in summer you know it depends  on the individual about which things are  more difficult we do flight training we  learn the Russian language since  everybody up here and the space station  speaks both English and Russian we learn  all about the different types of systems  onboard the international space station  since we have to be the people not just  living up here we also if we if