ll very very simple things  which of course you know in your brain  that it will operate that way but  because our entire as a species through  the entire evolution of the human  species we've always had this effect of  gravity and for our very short lifespans  as individuals we've always had  everything we've known has this effect  of gravity wear down is towards my feet  and if I let go of something it will  fall and really reinterpreting having  your brain relearn all of those things  up here just how how to eliminate that  factor really takes some time and it  does it is very interesting I think it  really shows how plastic the plasticity  of the human brain and how it can adapt  and change just the way that I navigated  around and how my brain adapted to the  new surroundings within a few weeks it  was seemed to be using a totally  different neural pathway in terms of  spatial awareness where instead of just  having down be where my feet is  sometimes during the course of the day I  might need to go and get something up  here so suddenly I'm up here on what  what would look like the ceiling and my  brain and the beginning would sort of do  this flip and suddenly I would think  this was was what I would think was down  again but really it was up from relative  where I was before but even after just a  couple weeks of being up here my brain  had seemed to use a different way of  interpreting that in order to spatially  orient myself and is really interesting  to see as a physiologist I would have  loved to have some kind of EEG or MRI  measurement of the human brain as  sting to life up here in the first few  weeks especially hi my name is Ashley  Nava and my question is can you  communicate with your family yes we can  actually and so it's similar to the way  that I'm talking to you right now you  know we can talk to our ground teams in  Houston using these communications  pathways but I can also just call my  family on a cell phone we use an IP  Phone so it's similar to how Skype would  work and it's a program on our laptops  where we can dial a phone and then we  also have weekly conferences with our  family members so we have a video chat  just like Skype or FaceTime once a week  with our family to help keep us in touch  with everybody that's back on the ground  hi my name is Gianna from Colton k-8 and  my question is before you got accepted  as an astronaut did you ever consider  any other career well I certainly did I  had a whole nother career and that is  thanks to Scripps Institution of  Oceanography and UC San Diego and all of  the mentors and colleagues that I had  during my time as a graduate student  there my time at Scripps was invaluable  they really it really taught me how to  think as a critical thinker how to form  and conduct something efficiently using  the scientific method and most  importantly relevant to my job today it  taught me a lot about working with the  team and I was working as a comparative  physiologist so I was studying animals  in particular animals that live in  extreme environments and trying to  understand how those animals can do the  incredible things they can I was  particularly intrigued by by animals  that live at extraordinary that live and  work at extraordinary depths so diving  animals like emperor penguins and  elephant seals and also high-altitude  animals so birds that migrate over the  tallest mountains on the planet and to  me it was just fascinating to understand  how these animals could live in these  very oxygen deprived to zones so diving  animals penguins seals they are air  breathing breath hold divers just like  you and me but they can drive for 30  minutes for an emperor penguin or two  hours for an elephant seal and it was  just very fascinating to me to try to  think how can they do it what it is is  it that's special and unique and how  have they adapted to enable that  extraordinary behavior so I was working  as a scientist in various universities  and academia conducting research with  animals in the wild and it was a very  fulfilling career but when the  opportunity came to have this childhood  dream job I couldn't pass this up there  are some parallels on earth and one of  those is an airplane effective  affectionately known as the vomit comet  and it is an airplane that flies in a  parabola shaped pattern and essentially  the pilot shuts off the engine at the  top of that parabola so that the plane  and everything isn't in it is in free  fall back to the earth which is  essentially exactly what we are doing up  here on the space station in orbit so  that in the airplane since you're not  quite up high enough away from the earth  and the effects of gravity you only  generate about thirty five seconds of  weightlessness but it does feel a lot  like it does appear just that it only  lasts for those 30-second periods  another thing that seems quite familiar  that is something that you can even do  there in San Diego is go out into the  ocean or go into a swimming pool and if  you go underwater and make yourself  neutrally buoyant so imagine if you have  some weight on you and you're Midway  suspended in the swimming pool so that  you're not moving up and not moving down  that feels a little bit like it but of  course you still have gravity when  you're underwater but that's what we do  to train for spacewalks we have a big  pool where we use the spacesuit in  exactly that way because it does have a  lot of similarities well unfortunately  Jessica our time is rapidly coming to a  close but we want to thank you so much  deepest gratitude to you for spending so  much of your valuable time with us here  in San Diego here at Scripps Institution  of Oceanography at UC San Diego it's  been a real joy and a treat and we can't  wait to go diving and surfing when you  come back here to La Jolla thank you so  much Jessica  thank you everybody is so wonderful to  be able to spend just a little bit of  time with you I only wish we had more  time but please follow along with my  adventure and everything that I'm  posting that's coming down from the NASA  social media and other accounts there  are lots of resources online thank you  and have a wonderful day  [Applause]  well that was amazing  before we break for the for the day I  want to extend a big thank you to the  chaperones to the parents to the  teachers we hope you come back many many  times to UC San Diego and maybe just  maybe if we're lucky we'll get you to  come here to be students here either as  undergraduates or as one of our many  wonderful graduate students so when you  exit there are some exhibits that you  can look at put on by some of the  students here at Scripps Institution of  Oceanography can look through  microscopes you can examine and learn  more about places that you heard about  today Antarctica and the oceans and all  sorts of other things here and it's been  it's been a great pleasure thank you to  the scripps staff for hosting us dr.  Margaret linen for being such a gracious  host and I hope you all enjoy the rest  of your time and please please come back  again visit us at Scripps and the Birch  Aquarium thank you so much have a great  day  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you

